      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, the opposition often stems from a profound ideological belief that public schools and their elected bureaucracies should have absolute control over the curriculum, teaching methods, and who is taught what.\n\nCritics argue that vouchers undermine the \"one class per school\" model and the collective socialization that happens in public settings, viewing it as an attack on community and shared civic values rather than just a budgetary issue.\n\nFurthermore, many opponents fear that entering the private sector creates a system where parents, not communities or states, dictate educational standards, which they see as a dangerous erosion of democratic accountability and equal access.",
